</figure>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.11510v1/images/PlotA.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/241;" width="429" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Open-ended generation quality versus web presence.<span id="S5.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
mDolly judge scores plotted against an approximate web-presence proxy based on Common Crawl bucketed into five equal-width bins. The trend highlights robustness in lower-web-presence languages relative to same-scale competitors.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Generative Tasks</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Consistently high performance on open-ended tasks</span> <a href="#S5.T5" title="In 5.2 Generative Tasks ‣ 5 Results: balanced performance across languages ‣ Tiny Aya:" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes the performance on generative benchmarks, accompanied with language confusion measures in <a href="#S5.T6" title="In 5.2 Generative Tasks ‣ 5 Results: balanced performance across languages ‣ Tiny Aya:" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Language-specific results including secondary metrics like language confusion and naturalness are detailed in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Generative benchmark results by language ‣ Tiny Aya:" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>. We find that <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tiny Aya</span> performs strongly in open-ended tasks, even more in non-technical domains (mDolly) than technical domains (mArenaHard).
Competitor models show large standard deviation in scores across languages likely due to missing language support,<span id="footnote13" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">13</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Gemma does not specify which 140 languages it covers so we can only guess it does not cover these outliers.</span></span></span> while <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tiny Aya</span> stands out with the highest naturalness ratings across both tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5.1 Discriminative Tasks ‣ 5 Results: balanced performance across languages ‣ Tiny Aya:" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> relates open-ended generation quality on mDolly to the languages approximate presence on the web (CommonCrawl page count):<span id="footnote14" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">14</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">14</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://commoncrawl.github.io/cc-crawl-statistics/plots/languages.csv" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://commoncrawl.github.io/cc-crawl-statistics/plots/languages.csv</a> page counts from 2026-04</span></span></span> While competitors suffer from a steep drop for less-dominant languages, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tiny Aya</span> provides a more stable performance across the bench. Disparities across languages and regions still remain, but <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tiny Aya</span> contributes to reducing them.</p>
</div>
